ntdll: name what consumed the stack, not only that it ran out.

`virtual_setup_exception` reports the moment a thread's stack ran out and nothing
whatever about what filled it. The address it prints is whichever frame was
unlucky enough to touch the last page, which is why the same line has now been
read seven times without answering anything:

  Resident Evil Requiem, 4 MB stack:
    stack overflow 1776 bytes addr 0x7ffff09bf0 stack 0x20910 (0x20000-0x21000-0x420000)

  VS Code Insiders, 8 MB stack, unchanged across Wine revisions 41 to 45:
    stack overflow 1664 bytes addr 0x6fffef9c34 stack 0x20980 (0x20000-0x21000-0x820000)

`0x6fffef9c34` is `prepare_exception_arm64ec` entry plus four -- its prologue,
resolved against the shipped `ntdll.dll` through the only ADRP/ADD pair in
`.text` that materialises patch 0040's format string. So a delivery was already
under way and could not allocate its 3280 bytes. That is the last frame, never
the one that spent the stack, and patch 0040's own counter -- which fires between
256 KB and 16 KB remaining -- printed nothing in any session, so nested delivery
did not spend it either.

Raising the floor does not answer it. 4 MB to 8 MB moved the wall and changed
nothing else, and 8 MB is not a Wine number: `Code - Insiders.exe` carries
`SizeOfStackReserve` 0x800000 in its own PE header, the figure Chrome, Edge and
Firefox all ship with. The thread had exactly what Windows would have given it.

At the unrecoverable branch every byte between StackLimit and StackBase is still
committed -- `virtual_alloc_thread_stack` maps the whole stack `VPROT_COMMITTED`
and only guards the bottom two pages, so a thread stack here never grows a page
at a time and there is no growth event to observe -- and it still holds the
frames that filled it. That residue is the only place the question can be
answered, and only for the few microseconds before `abort_thread`.

**What this measures, and why it is not a frequency count.** The first version of
this counted repeated words with a 16-slot Misra-Gries table and reported
garbage: counters that reached zero were decremented again and wrapped, giving
counts near 2^32 in the first live reading. Fixing the wrap would not have saved
it. Sixteen slots over a million words only guarantee items appearing more than
n/17 times -- about 62,000 -- while a recursion 2,000 frames deep leaves each
return address exactly 2,000 times. It was the wrong instrument for the quantity.

A stack has structure a histogram throws away: a recursion puts the same word at
a *constant interval*. Pass one records, for every repeated word, how far apart
the two occurrences sat, in a fixed table keyed by a hash of the value; the
commonest interval is the frame size. Pass two takes that interval back over the
residue and names the words recurring at exactly it. Stride and count together
are the reading -- N frames of S bytes -- which separates genuine recursion from
ordinary depth at an inflated cost per frame, and is comparable against what the
same call costs unemulated.

Verified against synthetic stacks with known answers before shipping, 8 MB of
pseudorandom junk with one planted recursion in it:

  2000 frames of 3280 bytes  -> interval 3280, planted address 2000 times
  1500 frames of 1360 bytes  -> interval 1360, planted address 1500 times
  100000 frames of 48 bytes  -> interval 48,   planted address 100000 times
  junk with no recursion     -> reports that nothing repeats

Static tables rather than stack ones, because this runs on a thread with no stack
left; the first dying thread takes them and any second is silent, which is the
right trade for a diagnostic. Addresses print raw -- `WINEDEBUG` carries
`+loaddll`, so every module's load address is already in the same log.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -5108,6 +5108,131 @@
 }
 
 
+/* Vessel: what the stack was full of, read before the thread is thrown away.
+ *
+ * `virtual_setup_exception` reports the moment a stack ran out and nothing about
+ * what filled it -- the address it prints is whichever frame was unlucky enough
+ * to touch the last page. For VS Code Insiders that address has been the same in
+ * seven sessions across five Wine revisions, and it names
+ * `prepare_exception_arm64ec`'s prologue: a delivery already under way that could
+ * not allocate its 3280 bytes. The last frame, never the one that spent the
+ * stack.
+ *
+ * At the unrecoverable branch every byte between StackLimit and StackBase is
+ * still committed and still holds the frames that filled it, so the question the
+ * overflow line cannot answer is answerable here and nowhere else.
+ *
+ * **What this measures, and why it is not a frequency count.** The first version
+ * of this counted repeated words with a 16-slot Misra-Gries table and reported
+ * garbage: counters that reached zero were decremented again and wrapped, and
+ * even with that fixed, 16 slots over a million words only guarantee items
+ * appearing more than n/17 times -- about 62,000 -- while a recursion 2,000
+ * frames deep leaves each return address exactly 2,000 times. It was the wrong
+ * instrument for the quantity being measured.
+ *
+ * A stack has structure a histogram throws away: a recursion puts the same word
+ * at a *constant interval*. So pass one records, for every repeated word, how far
+ * apart the two occurrences sat, in a fixed table keyed by a hash of the value.
+ * The most common interval is the frame size. Pass two takes that interval back
+ * over the residue and names the words that recur at exactly it. The pair of
+ * numbers -- stride and count -- is the reading: N frames of S bytes, which
+ * separates genuine recursion from ordinary depth at an inflated cost per frame,
+ * and is directly comparable against what the same call costs unemulated.
+ *
+ * Static tables rather than stack ones, because this runs on a thread that has
+ * no stack left. First dying thread takes them and any second is silent, which
+ * is the right trade for a diagnostic: one complete reading beats two interleaved
+ * ones. Addresses print raw -- `WINEDEBUG` carries `+loaddll`, so every module's
+ * load address is already in the same log and resolving them is offline work. */
+#define VESSEL_RESIDUE_SLOTS   4096u                    /* last-seen table, power of two */
+#define VESSEL_RESIDUE_STRIDES 4096u                    /* strides to 64 KB, 16-byte units */
+#define VESSEL_RESIDUE_NAMED   6u                       /* addresses named at the winning stride */
+#define VESSEL_RESIDUE_MAX     ((SIZE_T)64 * 1024 * 1024)
+
+static ULONG_PTR    vessel_seen_value[VESSEL_RESIDUE_SLOTS];
+static unsigned int vessel_seen_index[VESSEL_RESIDUE_SLOTS];
+static unsigned int vessel_stride_hits[VESSEL_RESIDUE_STRIDES];
+
+static void vessel_dump_stack_residue( const struct thread_stack_info *stack_info )
+{
+    static int taken;
+
+    const ULONG_PTR *base, *end;
+    unsigned int best_bucket = 0, best_hits = 0, named = 0, i;
+    SIZE_T words, stride, stride_words, at;
+
+    if (!__sync_bool_compare_and_swap( &taken, 0, 1 )) return;
+
+    base = (const ULONG_PTR *)stack_info->limit;
+    end  = (const ULONG_PTR *)stack_info->end;
+    if (base >= end) return;
+    if ((SIZE_T)((const char *)end - (const char *)base) > VESSEL_RESIDUE_MAX) return;
+    words = end - base;
+
+    ERR( "stack residue: %u live words from %p to %p\n",
+         (unsigned int)words, (const void *)base, (const void *)end );
+
+    /* pass one: how far apart does a repeated word sit? */
+    for (at = 0; at < words; at++)
+    {
+        ULONG_PTR v = base[at];
+        unsigned int h;
+        SIZE_T d;
+
+        if (v < 0x10000) continue;  /* not an address, and the commonest word on any stack */
+
+        h = (unsigned int)((v * 2654435761u) >> 11) & (VESSEL_RESIDUE_SLOTS - 1);
+        if (vessel_seen_value[h] == v)
+        {
+            d = (at - (SIZE_T)vessel_seen_index[h]) * sizeof(ULONG_PTR);
+            if (d && !(d & 15) && d / 16 < VESSEL_RESIDUE_STRIDES) vessel_stride_hits[d / 16]++;
+        }
+        vessel_seen_value[h] = v;
+        vessel_seen_index[h] = (unsigned int)at;
+    }
+
+    for (i = 1; i < VESSEL_RESIDUE_STRIDES; i++)
+        if (vessel_stride_hits[i] > best_hits) { best_hits = vessel_stride_hits[i]; best_bucket = i; }
+
+    if (!best_hits)
+    {
+        ERR( "stack residue: nothing repeats at any interval below %u bytes -- either this is\n"
+             "               not a recursion, or its frames are bigger than this can see\n",
+             (unsigned int)(VESSEL_RESIDUE_STRIDES * 16) );
+        return;
+    }
+
+    stride = (SIZE_T)best_bucket * 16;
+    stride_words = stride / sizeof(ULONG_PTR);
+    ERR( "stack residue: commonest interval %u bytes, seen %u time(s); %u bytes of stack is %u such frames\n",
+         (unsigned int)stride, best_hits,
+         (unsigned int)((const char *)end - (const char *)base),
+         (unsigned int)(((const char *)end - (const char *)base) / (stride ? stride : 1)) );
+
+    /* pass two: which words actually recur at that interval */
+    {
+        ULONG_PTR value[VESSEL_RESIDUE_NAMED];
+        unsigned int count[VESSEL_RESIDUE_NAMED];
+
+        for (at = 0; at + stride_words < words; at++)
+        {
+            ULONG_PTR v = base[at];
+            unsigned int j;
+
+            if (v < 0x10000 || base[at + stride_words] != v) continue;
+
+            for (j = 0; j < named; j++) if (value[j] == v) break;
+            if (j < named) { count[j]++; continue; }
+            if (named < VESSEL_RESIDUE_NAMED) { value[named] = v; count[named] = 1; named++; }
+        }
+
+        for (i = 0; i < named; i++)
+            ERR( "stack residue:   %p repeats %u time(s) at that interval\n",
+                 (void *)value[i], count[i] + 1 );
+    }
+}
+
+
 /***********************************************************************
  *           virtual_setup_exception
  */
@@ -5137,6 +5262,7 @@
